Z||000000||V8Dreamer||08-10-2001||08:38 AM||||Well, I did the front pads on my new GT in a little over an hour a few days ago(I work slow).  Last night at about 8:00 I decided to do the rears.....at 9:20 I still wasn't done with the first one, I got the pads installed and everything, I just can't get the ******* E-Brake cable and spring seated correctly.  Does anyone know of any tricks to getting this done?  I'm tired of going with the brute force method, as it isn't working too well for me so far.<P>TIA,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||08-10-2001||10:05 AM||||Did you loosen the handbrake adjuster?  If not, give that a try.||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Dreamer||08-10-2001||10:14 AM||||I'm assuming you are talking about loosening the cable where it runs along the cradle to make the "lengthen" the cable?  No I haven't yet, but I think I will tonight for sure.....any other suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000003||starfighter007@msn.com||08-10-2001||11:28 AM||||yes I agree loosen yhe cale at the cradle&lt;hook cable up re adjust and drive||||reg||7
Z||000004||maryjane||08-10-2001||12:25 PM||||Gotta loosen the adjuster allright. Ya know, I've looked at that ebrake cable setup a dozen times and it looks like the drivers side works backwards, looks like the cable is pushin on the lever, not pulling. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||08-10-2001||02:14 PM||||Loostening the cables helps, but I think Dreamerboy's problem is getting the spring in place. His wimpy little fingers can't compress the spring enough to get it in. At least that's what it seemed like when one of the springs went flying past my head last night!! Watch it Ryan, you'll put an eye out with one of those things!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> LMAO! Maybe if I'm in a better mood tonight I'll help you. Or maybe I'll finish it before you get here and do some "test" driving!! Oh this is going to be fun!||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||08-10-2001||02:17 PM||||Oh yeah, to answer your question, the easy way to replace the rear pads is to buy the proper tool to turn the pistons in without removing the park brake lever. But that's too easy. There is no easy way to put the springs back on.||||reg||1
